; ScrollContainer's clip rect and the scrollbars it draws for it.
;
; The one variable: what a ScrollContainer does to content that does not fit.
; The left container is 600x500 around a 900x900 child, so every band and block
; inside it is authored in one coordinate system and cut by exactly one
; boundary — the container's own rect. Three of the blocks STRADDLE that
; boundary rather than sitting wholly on one side of it (`StraddleRight` at the
; vertical edge, `StraddleBottom` at the horizontal one, `Outside` beyond both),
; which is what separates a real clip from a container that simply draws
; nothing past its child's visible corner: a missing clip leaves all three
; whole and paints them over the backdrop, and an over-eager one drops the
; straddlers entirely instead of halving them.
;
; The two bands run the full 900 in each axis so the cut is a long straight
; edge rather than a corner — a clip plane that is off by a pixel, or applied
; in the wrong axis, shows as a visibly displaced edge over that length.
;
; The right container is the same node type with a child SMALLER than its box,
; and is here for the bars: overflow is what decides whether a bar is drawn at
; all, so with no overflow it must show none, while the left one shows both,
; each dodging the other's reserved strip. Nothing else in the goldens draws a
; themed ScrollBar track or grabber, and nothing else clips at all — a
; ScrollContainer that stopped clipping, or one that drew bars unconditionally,
; would leave every other baseline untouched.
;
; Measured against Godot 4.6.3 at `--mode 2d`: the clip is pixel-exact. Nothing
; differs by more than a rounding step anywhere in the clipped content, in the
; frame right of the clip, in the frame below it, or in the fitting container —
; 711,360 pixels, zero disagreements. The bars land in the same 8 px strips and
; carry the same two colours where they cross the container's own background
; (track rgb(36, 38, 44), grabber rgb(124, 125, 128)). What differs is the order
; they compose in where a bar crosses CONTENT: Godot draws its bars last, so its
; grabber over the magenta block reads rgb(164, 133, 157) along y=300,
; x=712..719, while ours leaves the block's own rgb(217, 89, 191) there. That is
; 3,200 px and it is the whole of the disagreement; the baseline below is OURS.

[gd_scene format=3]

[node name="Root" type="Control"]
anchors_preset = 15
anchor_right = 1.0
anchor_bottom = 1.0

[node name="Backdrop" type="ColorRect" parent="."]
anchors_preset = 15
anchor_right = 1.0
anchor_bottom = 1.0
color = Color(0.1, 0.12, 0.16, 1)

[node name="OverflowSlot" type="ColorRect" parent="."]
